{
  "title": "Pangea™ Proximal Lateral Tibia Plate",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/implants/423213/pangea-tm--proximal-lateral-tibia-plate",
  "subtitle": "Proximal Third Tibia Fracture Updated: Jun 8 2025",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Related Implants",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Pangea™ series contains 5 proximal tibia plates",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**Extra Articular Proximal Tibia**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**Partial Articular Proximal Tibia**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**Proximal Lateral Tibia (this topic)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**Proximal Posteromedial Tibia**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**Proximal Medial Tibia**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Pangea Design",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Variable Angle Locking Technology**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423213/images/0e2737d1-25a3-4d2e-b8fc-2175db3ef195_screenshot_2025-01-07_at_10.54.49_am.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Pangea’s variable angle locking technology uses a CoCr locking screw, which is harder than the Ti6Al4V ELI plate, allowing for the screwhead’s threads to form a definitive plate-screw interface in the plate's locking hole by engaging the softer, Ti6Al4V material.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "This technology allows the user to aim and lock the screw into the plate within a true 30° cone of the predetermined hole trajectory. The variable angle drill guide provided with the system offers guidance with respect to the limit of the 30° cone. The locking mechanism remains functional for up to three attempts at locking screw insertion.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Circular universal holes accept non-locking screws, and locking screws within a 30° cone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**2.0mm Proximal Suture Holes with Undercuts**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Allows for suture threading after plate placement",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423213/images/c80ddabe-e28f-4db8-b04b-cfed688ac584_screenshot_2025-01-08_at_10.38.58_am.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Rafting Screws**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Designed to follow the angle of the tibial plateau to support the articular surface",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Monoaxial Hole**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "**2 Kickstand Screws**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Designed to provide support for the medial tibia plateau",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423213/images/c80ddabe-e28f-4db8-b04b-cfed688ac584_screenshot_2025-01-08_at_10.38.58_am.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Oblong Hole**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "**2.0mm K-Wire Hole**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "To provide temporary fixation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Placement",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "This plate is placed on the anterolateral surface of the proximal tibia",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Placed in the proper position when the proximal end of the plate is adjacent to the articular surface allowing for the proximal rafting screws to support the joint surface",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The Proximal Lateral Tibia plate is designed for use in bicondylar fractures and lateral unicondylar fractures",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Fit",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Proximal Lateral Tibia Plate is designed to aid with the treatment lateral split, depression, or split depression fractures and bicondylar fractures of the tibial plateau.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Designed with the use of SOMA: Stryker Orthopedics Modeling and Analytics.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "SOMA analysis shows better anatomic plate fit of the Proximal Lateral Tibia plate compared to Synthes large and small bend plates. 2",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Indications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Pangea Tibia Plating System is indicated for the internal fixation and stabilization of tibia bone fractures and osteotomies in normal and osteopenic bone, including:",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Diaphyseal, metaphyseal, epiphyseal, extra- and intra-articular fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Non-unions, malunions, and deformities",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Periprosthetic fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Approaches",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Surgeons may use a standard lateral or anterolateral approach to the proximal lateral tibia",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Technical specifications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Standard plate lengths: 2-16 hole (95-291mm)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Thickness: 3.3mm",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Left and right anatomic plate options",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Drill bits: Ø2.5mm x 135mm (542020) Ø2.5mm x 215mm (542021)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Screws",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Rafting Screws**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "The rafting screws are designed to follow the angle of the tibial plateau, reducing the potential for interference with the joint line at nominal angles.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Kickstand Screws**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Two kickstand screw options which are designed to allow for the capture of posteromedial fragments one posterior and one more central.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "This triangular fixation may increase the stability and support of the plate construct.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
